  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the safe return to school of children in the Philippines&#x2019; Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao (BARMM). After two years of offline classes due to pandemic restrictions, Pathways facilitated inclusive and participatory planning, public consultations, and awareness campaigns leading to the transition policy that guided the return to in-person classes in 2022. Australia also supported the BARMM Learning Recovery Strategy aimed at improving children&#x2019;s early literacy and numeracy skills and socioemotional learning post-pandemic.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s Education Pathways to Peace in Mindanao program helped 8,546 children access kindergarten to grade 2 education in communities without schools in the Philippines&#x2019; Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao (BARMM). For the 2022-23 school year, Australia supported community learning centre operations in 109 of 210 communities without schools. Of these children, 1,426 are Indigenous peoples, 132 were affected by local armed conflicts, and 30 were children with disability. Forty-nine per cent (4,180) were girls, of which 1,298 were in kindergarten.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In partnership with the Bangsamoro Women Commission, Australia facilitated coaching and mentoring for 1,920 Gender and Development (GAD) coordinators to strengthen gender-sensitive programming and budgeting in the Philippines&#x2019; autonomous Muslim Mindanao region. This helped unlock funds for gender-related initiatives. As reported in MBHTE&#x2019;s 2024 GAD Accomplishment Report, GAD fund utilisation rose from 0.6% in 2023 to 4.2% in 2024, partly due to these expanded activities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through Pathways, Australia has strengthened the ability of the Ministry of Basic, Higher and Technical Education (MBHTE) of the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao (BARMM) to deliver more inclusive education services. Australia&#x2019;s supported the drafting and adoption of the Omnibus Inclusive Schools Approach (ISA) Policy Guidelines, which institutionalises inclusion strategies and practical tools and processes. These include the Bangsamoro Child Finding Tool and the Reach Every Child Campaign, which enable the Ministry to identify out of school children across the BARMM.
&#x200d;
Australia&#x2019;s support specifically led to the incorporation of a localised and simplified version of the Washington Group/UNICEF Child Functioning Module in school enrolment and registration, allowing for early detection of student disabilities, proactive resource allocation and design of targeted interventions. Australia also strengthened MBHTE&#x2019;s referral system for children with special needs and functional difficulties to local government services that can provide further medical diagnosis and assistance.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under the Education Pathways to Peace in Mindanao Program, Australia supported the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region of Muslim Mindanao (BARMM) Government to expand its education service delivery, including to those marginalised due to their ethnicity, religion, disability or geographic isolation. Through the project, Australia helped the BARMM Government deliver kindergarten to grade 3 education in 128 isolated communities without schools. Australia also supported the Government to institutionalise an annual child finding initiative. Through this initiative, 1,865 out-of-school children were identified and assisted with their school enrolment.  Australia also helped establish the Bangsamoro Education Management Information System (BEMIS) which now contains records for over 1.2 million learners enrolled in 4,635 basic, higher and vocational education institutions. These initiatives contribute to effective and accountable governance, by providing data to inform evidence-based inclusive policies and service provision.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
